WEBVTT

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:06.480
Manaka, it looked like you chased 
after us in the heat of the moment.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.580
Don't tell me you came 
without telling anyone.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.610
No, I asked my parents 
and got their permission.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.980
I also asked Suzu-san for 
an advance on my wages,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.940
and Monroe-senpai and Sakura-senpai 
helped me book the flight.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.460
That was audacious of you.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.960
I was surprised at myself, too.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:30.710
But I regretted not doing anything 
myself when my father was transferred.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.660
Why did you keep it a secret, though?

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.100
That was Suzu-san's suggestion.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.870
She said Sensei would 
definitely oppose the idea,

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.970
and if I'd told you, you would've told her.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.850
That was good advice.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.960
You're all so young.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.470
Though, if you had actually 
asked me beforehand,

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.910
you could have accompanied 
us as an observer.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.530
You'd still have to pay for 
accommodation yourself,

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.760
but it was in the application notes.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.040
Well, you're here now, so you should 
try asking as nicely as you can.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.060
I'll support you as best I can, too.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.400
Thank you—Ow!

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.270
Are you okay?!

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.670
<i>I really should read 
important notes more.</i>

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.010
<i>I feel so much better with Ao here, though.</i>

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.690
<i>I wonder what the other 
participants are like.</i>

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.480
<b>Ishigaki Youth Center</b>

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.440
11. Shining Star Challenge!

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.400
<b>Ishigaki 
Youth Center</b>

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.400
<b>Today's Educational Groups</b>

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.400
<b>Welcome</b>

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.400
Shining
 Star 
Challenge

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.840
<b>Ishigaki Island Star Festival</b>

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.840
Amane Neon

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.290
Once you finish checking in, 
please wait in the training room.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:08.820
Ao.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.660
Yeah.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.600
Excuse me.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:12.400
Yes?

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.280
I-I'm Manaka.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.060
I failed the written screening,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.840
but would it be possible to at least observe?

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.300
My Feelings About
Space and the Stars

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.300
What's this?

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.360
I organized the portion that wouldn't 
fit in my written application.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.330
That's heavy!

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.700
Sorry about my student.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.520
Is the organizer around?

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.780
Yes, over there.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.400
Hirose-san! It's been a long time.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.240
Hey.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.340
Huh?

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.990
Hirose-san has been organizing 
this event since I attended.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.450
<b>Konohata Mira</b>

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.450
<b>Makita Shiho</b>

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:54.420
Oh, so this is her?

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.500
The handful you mentioned on the phone.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.820
The handful?

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.530
The handful?

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.710
I'm sorry for being unreasonable.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:06.640
No, I'm just surprised. I've never seen 
a student as gutsy as her before.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.890
Well, I can't send you packing 
after you came this far.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.190
Here.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.100
Huh? Then...

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.600
Thank you so much!

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.190
Sorry about that.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:25.200
Don't worry about it. It's our job 
to help children pursue their dreams.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.820
I'm so glad!

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:28.580
Yeah!

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.500
She said she got permission to observe!

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.600
Good for her!

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.090
There's no way they'd send her back after 
she went all the way to Ishigaki Island.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:43.020
You were actually worried about her 
the whole time. You're so cute.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.470
Hey, Chikage!

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:50.370
It's been a whole year.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:53.730
I'm happy I can come here with you again.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.060
Wha... I'm just chaperoning 
their summer camp as an alumnus.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:59.970
We're not here to have fun.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.280
Sure.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:04.850
So this is a life-size artificial satellite?

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.240
Yes! I was so impressed when I saw it

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.280
for the first time at our 
summer camp last year!

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.060
You and I may be part of the geology group,

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.620
but I think learning about 
astronomy like this is fun, too.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:16.350
Yes.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.530
Mira and Ao were so enthusiastic about 
asking everyone here about asteroids.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:26.470
Yeah, they held up the guide and 
peppered her with questions.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:28.600
They really love them, huh?

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.260
But going there without 
warning was too reckless.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:33.600
You mean Ao-senpai?

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:39.780
She failed the screening, 
so she shouldn't be barging in.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.110
You may be right, Nana-san.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.690
What she did certainly doesn't merit praise.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:52.150
Why did you all support her, then?

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.300
Over the past year, those two 
have been making strides toward

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.650
their dream of discovering an asteroid.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:03.800
They gave it all they had, never accepting
that it was beyond their reach.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:07.560
They even managed to move in together, 
which everyone thought was reckless,

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.660
and now they finally have this chance.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:11.740
Isolation...

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.770
But they should take action
 and move forward.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:24.110
Seeing Ao-san refuse to give up makes me 
want to do all I can right now, too.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:29.820
All I can do right now...

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.660
<b>Motivation</b>

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.660
<b>Courage</b>

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.660
<b>Guts</b>

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:33.680
Welcome to Ishigaki Island.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.660
I'm your counselor, Hanashima.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.290
Let's start by having everyone 
introduce themselves.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.190
We'll start with you.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.420
I'm Satou, a third-year.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.760
I'm president of the science club.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.010
Nice to meet you all.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.340
<i>There are several people here who 
aren't from earth science clubs.</i>

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.160
I'm Tomori Asuka, a second-year! 
Astronomy club!

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.600
I came here from Honto. I love idols!

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:57.630
Huh?

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.950
I'm Makita Shiho, and I'm from Kyushu.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.940
I like people.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:05.930
Huh?

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.220
I'm Konohata Mira, a second-year!

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:12.870
I'm vice president of our earth sciences 
club, and I like Saturn and Cetus!

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.700
<i>They make Mira seem normal.</i>

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.160
<b>Shining Star 
Challenge</b>

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:21.300
<i>The Shining Star Challenge, 
or Shiny Challenge, would take three days.</i>

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.540
<i>There would be lectures, 
tours of the observatory,</i>

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:27.200
<i>observations, presentations 
of our results, and much more!</i>

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.700
Oh, hey, is that the
limited-edition Kanto one?

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:36.140
Yeah. I brought it with me so I wouldn't 
get sleepy during observation,

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.310
but I'm so nervous, I haven't slept much.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.430
Would you like one, Tomorin?

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:44.050
Tomorin? I like that nickname.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:45.820
Thanks for the coffee!

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:47.940
Whoa, it's sweet!

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.680
Miracchi, do you have a sweet tooth?

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.870
Huh? No, not really.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.870
<i>She's making friends already.</i>

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.060
You sure?

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.060
<i>Wow.</i>

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:56.210
Um, do you have a moment?

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:57.780
<i>It's the girl who likes people.</i>

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.800
I heard your exchange at the check-in desk.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.870
You're here even though 
you failed the screening?

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.630
Yes. I couldn't just give up on it, 
so I followed in secret.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.270
Wow. You seem so docile, 
but you're pretty bold.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:14.340
I bet your brain is interesting.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:15.550
My brain?

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.680
We're heading out for the tour.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:20.710
Tell me the details later, okay?

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:24.270
<i>What is she going to do to me?</i>

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.480
This is the VERA Ishigaki Station.

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:30.190
Wow, it's huge!

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.970
The one used by the Geospatial 
Information Authority of Japan?

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:34.400
You sure know your stuff.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:38.370
It's a similar antenna to the 
one that used to be in Tsukuba.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:41.410
There are four of these antennas
 located throughout Japan,

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:46.420
Mizusawa

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:46.420
Iriki

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:46.420
Ogasawara

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:46.420
Ishigaki Island

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:46.420
and together, they create a radio telescope 
that's 2,300 kilometers in diameter.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:48.720
This is a telescope?

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.310
Where do you look into it?

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:51.860
You don't look with your eyes.

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.880
You use radio signals and look at the data.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.550
Huh? And you can see stars that way?

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.210
Makita-chan, are you a space case?

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:02.350
I do get called that sometimes, but I'm not.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.360
The VERA mainly observes maser bodies 
that emit strong electromagnetic signals,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.560
to try and create a 3-D map of the Milky Way.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:11.320
A map?

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.570
Maser Source

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.570
Distance

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.570
Stellar 
Parallax

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.570
Relative VLBI

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.570
Earth

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.570
1 AU

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.570
Sun

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:15.090
We triangulate the astral 
bodies in space using their

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:18.570
stellar parallax to determine 
their precise position and motion.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.520
So this time, we're going to have

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:25.330
the electromagnetic group use VERA 
to try and pinpoint a new maser object.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.510
<i>They can make maps of space, too.</i>

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:31.040
<i>I should tell Ino-senpai about this.</i>

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.760
Wow, it's so big!

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.720
And it's kind of cute!

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:41.600
The Murikabushi Telescope 
has a 105-centimeter aperture.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.680
It's the largest reflecting 
telescope in Kyushu, Okinawa.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:47.350
Murky bush?

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.690
Murikabushi. It's the local 
name for the Pleiades.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:55.130
Here, we conduct observations 
using visible and infrared light.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:58.150
If you're hunting for asteroids, 
this is the one to use.

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:03.430
By "reflecting telescope," do they mean 
it uses mirrors rather than lenses?

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:08.710
Well, uh... the light reflects off the 
primary mirror and the secondary mirror...

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:12.220
Murikabushi is a Cassegrain 
reflector telescope.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:16.070
It uses a primary concave mirror and a 
secondary convex mirror to form the image.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:19.410
It has three focal lenses for 
research and observation, too.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.840
You know a lot about telescopes!

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.460
I've never seen the stars
 through a telescope, though.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:26.780
Huh?

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:32.610
<b>Electromagnetic Group</b>

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:32.610
<b>Optical Group</b>

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.000
Now that we're finished with the tour,

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.610
we'll be splitting into the electromagnetic 
group and the optical group.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:36.860
Each group will get to try searching 
for celestial objects themselves.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:39.930
I hope we get along, Tomorin, Makki!

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.410
Me, too.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.740
Makki?

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.510
We'll begin our observations after dinner.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.990
Make sure you eat up and give it your all.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:49.460
Okay!

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.770
Huh? Ao and Sensei are gone.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.180
Now that you mention it, so is my teacher.

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:57.920
Oh, I got a message.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:59.860
They said they're grabbing dinner.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.640
They don't provide meals for us, after all.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:06.400
It's my treat tonight, so eat up.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.100
Thank you.

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:11.570
<i>I need to eat quick,
 since observation starts soon.</i>

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:16.280
Yuki-chan, sorry for the wait!

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.110
Oh, over here!

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:21.220
<i>Oh, that's the lady from the hall...</i>

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:23.820
Makita's chaperone, Hayakawa.

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:28.160
She was in my group when I took 
part in the Shiny Challenge.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:32.640
Man, to think we'd both
 end up becoming teachers.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.410
I never would have imagined it back then.

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:36.920
Me neither.

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:39.420
It's kind of fun though, isn't it?

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.930
Getting to eat hamburgers 
together here again.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.260
I figured you'd be drinking root beer.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:48.240
How can I not drink it when I'm here?

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.720
True.

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:52.150
Ah, that's good!

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:52.610
<i>This is going to take a while...</i>

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:53.570
Want one, Kayo?

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:54.870
I had some earlier.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:08.470
<i>Our optical group went to the observatory 
to make our observations, but...</i>

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.440
That was rough, right after dinner.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.420
That was a really winding road.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.510
If you're not feeling well, 
do want to lie down for a bit?

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:22.760
We have a nap room here for the researchers.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.010
A researcher's bed!

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:26.440
I wonder what it's like.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:27.390
I want to try it out!

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.600
You girls are so excited.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:37.840
Now, where do you think the 
asteroids we're after are located?

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:39.490
The asteroid belt.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:39.490
The asteroid belt!

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:41.030
Oh, a duet.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:42.620
That's right.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:45.780
So, what region of the sky 
should we be observing, then?

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:47.150
The region in opposition!

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:48.740
This time they're in unison.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.950
That's the region where it's 
closest to Earth, right?

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:52.850
Right.

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.460
Horiguchi-san, if you would.

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:56.210
Sure.

00:12:56.230 --> 00:13:05.470
<b>Sun                       Earth               Opposition</b>

00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:59.520
As Konohata-san said, 
this is the region in opposition.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.220
Asteroids reflect the light from the sun,

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:05.470
so they're brightest when 
they're in opposition.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:08.330
So it's like when the idol 
comes front and center

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:11.060
on the stage to bathe in the spotlight?

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:12.680
What are you talking about?

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:17.350
The observatories and space telescopes 
throughout the world have already

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:19.840
discovered most of the bright objects 
with their full sky surveys.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:24.670
Most of the undiscovered asteroids 
are dimmer than magnitude 20-21.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.410
<b>Asteroid</b>

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.410
<b>Sun            Earth       Opposition</b>

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:25.920
Survey?

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:27.430
Astronomical surveys.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:28.960
Astronomical?

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:32.000
They observe a wide region all at once.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:33.180
Wow.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:37.750
<b>Astronomical</b>

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:34.980
By the way, here's how it's written.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:37.770
Next, we'll explain how to 
conduct your observations.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:41.940
Wait, am I the only one getting 
left behind here? Panic!

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.020
What are you doing?

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:46.740
I want a partner, too!

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:47.970
Come with me!

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:48.680
Huh?

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.890
S-Sure!

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:57.850
This time, we'll be marking each 
0.2 view of angular diameter

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:00.590
as a single section of 
the region in opposition.

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:04.420
<i>The moon fills an angular 
diameter of 0.5, so...</i>

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.530
<b>0.5 angular diameter</b>

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.530
<b>Moon</b>

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:06.700
<i>it's about this big?</i>

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:13.410
<b>the order we'll 
observe in</b>

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:09.650
We'll be taking five shots per section

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:13.410
with a six-minute exposure time each, 
so that's 30 minutes to photograph each one.

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:17.120
We'll be conducting our observations 
this way for as long as time permits.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:21.980
I-I'm just so happy to be learning 
an astronomer's know-how in person!

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.460
Until now, it's all been 
through books or the Internet.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:26.080
I know!

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:28.850
Going to see concerts live is the best!

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:30.430
<i>Their conversation actually meshed.</i>

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:33.340
Once we reduce the noise 
from our photograph data,

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.390
we analyze the photos with 
our asteroid survey software.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.640
We create a continuous 
display of those five images

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.230
by overlapping the fixed stars, and...

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:46.020
The objects in motion are your asteroids.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.240
There are so many in a single section!

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:50.410
There are a lot more than I imagined.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:54.200
The ones with red marks on them 
are the currently known asteroids.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:57.770
Blue marks appear on the ones that are 
potentially new, undiscovered asteroids.

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:00.710
Huh... looks pretty hard to find one.

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:04.080
Not to mention, you need at least

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:07.430
two different observations of a 
new asteroid, if I remember right.

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:12.320
Huh?! Then you're saying we're completely 
finished if we can't find one tonight?!

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:15.220
I-If you do happen to find 
a potential discovery,

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:17.560
I'll take charge of the
 follow-up observation.

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:20.520
There's another factor making 
observation difficult.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.300
Oh, a weather monitor.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:28.530
I was worried about that. The cloud 
patterns definitely aren't looking good.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:29.720
Oh, no...

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:31.940
In that case, let's hurry up and get started!

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.200
It's better to have as 
many chances as possible!

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:35.660
Yeah!

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:39.570
All right. Let's start loading 
up the photo program.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:46.590
Thank you so much for taking 
such good care of us again.

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:50.370
You're Yuki's precious students, after all.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.720
We're happy that you visited again.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:55.860
Does he love astronomy, too?

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.200
Now that you mention it, he's been 
wearing that this whole time.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:01.890
That was a souvenir our teacher 
bought for him at JAXA last year.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:05.610
Huh?

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:08.290
What? Is it from the girls?

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:11.040
Yes. They said the weather 
is bad down there,

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.420
so their observation isn't going well.

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:17.860
After they finally got this chance 
to realize their dream, too...

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.180
Senpai, use these!

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.630
Oh, you mean to make teruteru bouzu dolls?

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:30.710
Yes! Let's make enough
 to reach Ishigaki Island!

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:33.970
Oh, I'll make some, too.

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:49.230
Goodness, we sure did make a lot.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:50.960
It's not enough.

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:52.440
We should make more!

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:57.870
Our fourth observation...

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:02.040
They're all known objects.

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:05.610
Hey, it looks like the 
weather's getting worse.

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:07.790
Go faster!

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:08.900
Now, now.

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:09.990
Sensei!

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:12.840
Sometimes there's no point in panicking.

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:15.270
Let's have some tea 
and wait for it to clear up.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:16.720
There are so many sweets!

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.290
H-Having snacks this late?

00:17:21.370 --> 00:17:23.570
The guilty pleasure only 
makes them taste better!

00:17:23.570 --> 00:17:25.190
Oh, we have cup noodles, too.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:30.150
Wow. You're both former 
participants in the Shiny Challenge?

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:33.610
Yeah. The weather was like this then, too.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:38.780
Yuki-chan was in such a panic, 
determined to produce results.

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:41.080
No, it wasn't that bad.

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:44.540
Yuki-chan used to have 
a very serious attitude.

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:47.620
Like she was straining herself for something.

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:50.040
This is how stiff her expression used to be.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:52.630
You mean our Endou-sensei?

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:55.340
I have photos of you 
from back then right here.

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:58.720
You, too?! I'm so embarrassed!

00:17:59.030 --> 00:17:59.920
She's so young.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.280
Sensei, your expression's so stiff!

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:03.970
This is Hayakawa-sensei...

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:06.480
Who's this?

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:09.100
Oh, that's Nishina-chan.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:11.250
She's an astronomer overseas now.

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:12.340
Wow!

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:14.250
She really was an incredible girl.

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.170
She knew so much.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:19.360
Not just about astronomy,
but about history and literature, too.

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:23.870
She just had so much fun learning new things.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:26.830
She's the one who got me into it, too.

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:31.960
Wow.

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.630
I didn't discover any asteroids in the end,

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:41.050
but I got something even better out of it.

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:45.010
Honestly, the interactions we have 
with others are so important.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:48.730
Those encounters can be enough 
to change a person's world.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:52.520
You kind of seem more mature 
than usual right now.

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.270
She's truly a good teacher, huh?

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:59.200
E-Enough about us! You should 
go interact with your peers!

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.910
Hanashima-san, I'm borrowing the nap room!

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.790
She's just as cute as ever.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:07.370
I'll be in the nap room, too.

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:09.930
Hey, Manaka-san, I forgot to ask.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.080
Why did you come all the way here?

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:16.210
You have a lot of guts to come
 observe us by yourself.

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:19.540
I made a promise to Mira 
when we were children.

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:22.340
We're going to discover an 
asteroid and name it after her.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:26.830
To make that happen, I need to gather 
a lot more knowledge and experience.

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:30.060
Wow. What a big, romantic dream!

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:35.420
I'm sad it wasn't because 
you heard voices from space.

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:37.410
<i>Why is she disappointed?</i>

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:39.280
Why are you participating, Makki?

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:44.020
I came here to expand my horizons 
and meet all kinds of new people.

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:48.430
It's not directly related to astronomy, 
but I'm trying to become a counselor.

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:50.460
<i>That explains the comment about my brain.</i>

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:53.220
I wanted to meet Neon!

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:54.120
Neon?

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:56.790
The idol who loves astronomy, Amane Neon!

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:59.410
They hold a Star Festival after 
the Shiny Challenge, right?

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:01.080
She's this year's guest!

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:02.360
I get to see Neon in person!

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:04.480
I might be able to get close to her!

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:07.220
Everyone really is here 
for different reasons.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:11.600
Sorry, everyone, but it doesn't look 
like the weather will clear up tonight.

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:13.910
Let's call it a night.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:15.310
What? But...

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:19.920
Instead, why don't we practice looking 
for them using past photo data?

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:21.490
O-Okay!

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:23.710
What would you like to do, Manaka-san?

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.660
You could return to the dorms with 
Endou-san and the others, if you'd like.

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:31.120
No! Please let me observe until the very end!

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:58.940
Looks like the weather cleared 
up on Ishigaki Island!

00:20:58.940 --> 00:20:59.880
Really?!

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:01.720
Th-That's good.

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:05.860
I wonder if we helped make that happen.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:08.830
Your feelings made it through, Nana-chan.

00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:10.610
Yeah.

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:13.290
Here you go.

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:16.000
Oh, are you heading out already?

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:17.120
Yes.

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:19.670
Hey.

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:21.510
What are we doing about 
the afternoon observation?

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:24.300
Yeah, let's figure that out now.

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:26.400
Electromagnetic observation is nice, 
since it's not affected by the time.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.330
I guess we're all studying 
during the day today.

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:31.470
I think I'll need to drink a lot of coffee.

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:34.890
<b>Guava 
Juice</b>

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:36.790
Here. Stay hydrated.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:38.270
Thank you.

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:39.860
Try not to push yourself too hard.

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:42.580
It's going to be hot and sunny today.

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:43.820
<i>Sunny?</i>

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:47.750
I'm not sleepy! I'm not sleepy!

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:49.820
Are you okay? You're spacing out.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:50.990
Oh, yeah!

00:21:53.060 --> 00:21:54.200
<i>It's okay.</i>

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:55.660
<i>We still have a chance.</i>

00:21:58.140 --> 00:21:59.360
<i>Tonight, it'll happen.</i>

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.050
<i>I'm sure!</i>

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:40.060
12. 
Connected Cosmos

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:38.780
Next time, "Connected Cosmos."
